🎯 Attack Logic (The Hacker's Trick)
The Blueprint: The attacker distributes phishing documents disguised as standard Office files (.docx/.doc). 
These files contain no malicious VBA or XLM macros, successfully bypassing automated email gateways 
and signature-based antivirus scanners that only hunt for macro code.

The Mechanism: The exploit leverages a critical vulnerability in the MSHTML (Internet Explorer) rendering 
engine embedded within Microsoft Office. Inside the document's structure, the attacker embeds a malicious 
External OLE Relationship. When a user opens the document, Office forces MSHTML to covertly contact a 
remote server, download a weaponized HTML payload (containing obfuscated JavaScript), and execute arbitrary 
code on the victim's machine.

Evasion Technique: In this specific campaign, the attacker used extension spoofing 
(e.g., saving a DOCX file structure but naming it .doc) to confuse defenders and static analysis tools.

🕵️‍♂️ Investigation Process (How to Spot It)
MIME/Type Verification: Inspect file extensions against their true underlying headers to detect extension 
masquerading.
Macro Validation: Run automated scanners to rule out standard macro-based delivery mechanisms.
Archive Extraction: Unzip the Office Open XML structure (.docx) to manually audit hidden configuration profiles
and external resource mapping files.

Relationship Inspection: Parse the XML relationship definitions (.rels) to hunt for unauthorized, non-standard
external links (IOCs) fetching remote code.

